    31	## 4. Watcher coverage and gaps
    32	
    33	**Finding.** Current `auto.py` polls at 60 s, calls `poll_workers`, `poll_harness`, `check_limit`, then delivers events (`auto.py:68-79,1894-1945`). `poll_workers` treats server `retry` separately, waits two idle polls before `handle_result`, and reads passive adopted sessions through DB (`auto.py:1401-1455`). `check_limit` recognizes worker/MQ usage limits, holds new work and the queue, notifies, and clears after two clean polls (`auto.py:1515-1589`). `handle_doom` sends one corrective continue, then blocks a second guard stop (`auto.py:1376-1398`). `note_master_hold` alerts only when queued watcher events meet a master in provider retry for two polls; it deliberately ignores a merely busy master (`auto.py:1767-1792`). These cover the later doom episode and status-visible limits, but the 27 Oct smoke run shows the older watcher missed an internally retrying, apparently BUSY session (`REPORT.md:14-17`); **production efficacy of the current retry status path remains UNVERIFIED**.
    34	
    35	`check_stall` emits only when **every** active owned worker's newest activity is older than 20 minutes; its activity clock includes last send, last assistant message, and latest OUT mtime (`auto.py:1480-1512`). A single idle worker beside active peers is invisible. `check_gap` detects watcher silence over 10 minutes only when the watcher gets another poll (`auto.py:1712-1718`); it could have reported both shared freezes after 02:50 and 07:50, but could not wake the watcher while it was silent. The old run produced neither stall nor gap event during its 05:00-07:50 freeze (`REPORT.md:30,80,88`). The current `poll_workers` also reports an idle assistant as `done` after two polls without consulting a detached-job manifest (`auto.py:1431-1445`), and `handle_result` marks a nonerror response done without verifying the expected deliverable (`auto.py:1346-1358`). `ContextOverflowError` falls into the generic nonretry error path (`auto.py:553-565,1359-1367`); 6740572 needed an external continuation.
    36	
    37	**Exact catch rules.** (a) If an owned worker has no new assistant tool start/completion **and** no OUT or job heartbeat for 20 minutes while its expected deliverable is missing, emit `WORKER_STALL <sha>` even when other workers progress; do not flag a known live long tool or a job whose heartbeat advances. (b) If an idle/errored worker has a verified child manifest entry still alive, emit `JOB_RUNNING_WITH_IDLE_WORKER`, preserve the job, and defer `done` until the completion sentinel/result is read. (c) If a worker is `busy` with an empty assistant for two polls and the worker-server retry state or local provider log identifies a limit, apply `LIMIT`; otherwise emit `UNKNOWN_RETRY` without holding the queue. (d) On `ContextOverflowError`, emit `CONTEXT_STOP` and allow one checkpoint-based continuation after re-reading TODO/LOG; a second error stops. (e) On `check_gap` over 10 minutes, record the exact elapsed gap and all workers with missing deliverables before any wind-down decision; an **independent** watchdog is needed if detection must occur while `auto.py` itself is silent. Each rule is narrower than “busy for a long time” and maps respectively to the individual stall blind spot, orphan jobs, smoke retry, 6740572, and the two shared freezes.
    38	
    39	**What to change / expected gain / risk / check.** Implement the rules near `auto.py:1401-1512,1712-1718,1894-1945`. Expected gain: minutes to hours of earlier detection when the watcher is alive, and accurate post-gap accounting; no guaranteed rescue while the whole runtime is paused. Risks: false alerts on legitimate long tools, stale file mtimes, and duplicate continuations. Replay the saved DB/event timelines with a fake clock: one live worker plus one 21-minute idle worker must alert; a heartbeat job must not; the 23:19 and 04:59 gaps must each yield one GAP on return; the 6740572 error must continue once only; retry clean-up must leave the queue held until two clean polls. External watchdog installation is a separate operational decision.
